[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Declaration Files (.d.ts) and Module Augmentation in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript relies heavily on declaration files with the '.d.ts' extension to understand the shape of existing JavaScript libraries. Module augmentation allows developers to safely extend or modify existing types from external modules without altering the original source code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Declaration File?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A declaration file contains only type information, not implementation code. When you write standard TypeScript code, it compiles down to JavaScript. In contrast, '.d.ts' files are used solely for type checking and IntelliSense during development, and they are stripped out during the build process."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// custom.d.ts
declare module "my-legacy-library" {
    export function doSomething(options: { debug: boolean }): void;
    export const VERSION: string;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The Need for Module Augmentation"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you might use a popular third-party library where you need to add custom properties to an existing interface or module. Directly editing the library's type definitions is dangerous because package updates will overwrite your changes. Module augmentation solves this by letting you merge your custom types into the existing module definitions."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation of Module Augmentation"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your project includes the target module you wish to augment."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a new '.d.ts' file in your project (for example, 'express.d.ts' or 'window.d.ts')."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'declare module' syntax to target the exact path or package name of the module."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// augmentations.d.ts
import "express";

declares module "express" {
    interface Request {
        user?: {
            id: string;
            roles: string[];
        };
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Declaration Files"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include your custom '.d.ts' files in your 'tsconfig.json' under the 'include' array or ensure they are picked up automatically."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When augmenting modules, make sure you import the module at the top of the file to switch from a global script context to a module context."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using 'any' when augmenting types; maintain strict typing for robust code safety."}]
